<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/advanced/myth-ritual-symbol">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Myths and rituals are <strong>religion's working machinery</strong>: narratives that found
    worlds, ceremonies that move people through life's transitions, symbols that compress entire
    theologies into one image. The scholars in this lesson (Eliade, Turner, Campbell — and their
    critics) built the vocabulary every comparative study now uses.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="myth defined, ritual enacted, the sacred manifested, symbols decoded" widget="equationStep" config={mythCfg} />

  <h2>The theories and their critics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong> Campbell's monomyth ("hero with a thousand faces", 1949):</strong> one deep story pattern (departure–initiation–return) under myths worldwide. Influential on film-making; criticised by folklorists for cherry-picking — a cautionary tale about universal patterns.</li>
    <li><strong>Turner's structure and anti-structure:</strong> rituals create liminal states where normal hierarchy suspends — communitas. pilgrimages, festivals and raves all fit the frame.</li>
    <li><strong>Symbol systems are learned:</strong> Durkheim argued symbols carry SOCIETY (the totem is the group worshipping itself). Whether or not you buy it, the insight that symbols do social work is permanent.</li>
    <li><strong>Why the analytic tools matter now:</strong> political movements deploy myth (founding stories), rituals (rallies) and symbols (flags) — the analytical vocabulary is also media literacy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Eliade, The Sacred and the Profane (1957); Turner, The Ritual Process (1969); Campbell (1949); Durkheim, The Elementary Forms (1912).</p>
</TopicPage>
